---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { OB_ISSN } from "~/lib/catalogs";
---
<Base title="About" description="About the ITU Operational Bulletin.">
  <header class="mb-10">
    <p class="uppercase font-semibold" style="font-family: var(--font-sans); font-size: var(--step--1); letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--accent);">
      About
    </p>
    <h1 class="mt-1" style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--step-5); font-weight: 700;">
      The Operational Bulletin
    </h1>
  </header>

  <article class="prose-bulletin measure drop-cap">
    <p>
      The ITU Operational Bulletin (OB) is a biweekly cross-bureau publication
      issued jointly by the Telecommunication Standardization Bureau (TSB) and
      the Radiocommunication Bureau (BR) of the International Telecommunication
      Union (ITU). It disseminates operational information required by
      administrations, recognized operating agencies, and the wider
      international telecommunication community.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each edition is identified by a sequential issue number and groups its
      content into two parts: <em>general information</em> — running annexes,
      approved Recommendations, signalling code assignments, numbering plan
      notices, and miscellaneous communications — and <em>amendments to
      service publications</em>, which modify the structured lists annexed to
      the Bulletin (carrier codes, signalling codes, numbering plans and so on).
    </p>

    <h2>What you'll find here</h2>
    <p>
      This website presents the full run of the Bulletin — every issue from
      its current series onwards — together with the current snapshot of every
      annexed dataset and a complete change log per dataset. Each publication
      type carries its own cross-issue view, and every ITU-T Recommendation
      referenced from the Bulletin has a dedicated page listing the issues in
      which it was approved.
    </p>

    <h2>Identifier conventions</h2>
    <p>
      Issues are numbered with sequential integers (e.g. No. 1000). Datasets
      are addressed by filesystem slug (e.g. <code class="num">m1400-icc</code>,
      <code class="num">e118-iin</code>, <code class="num">q708-sanc</code>).
      Amendments apply an <em>action</em> — addition, suppression, replacement,
      modification, lapse, or deletion — to a position identified by a code
      (a country code, a carrier code, a signalling point code, and so on).
    </p>

    <h2>Source and provenance</h2>
    <p>
      The data shown here is drawn from the ITU's authoritative source
      repository, normalized into a per-issue and per-dataset shape, and
      rendered as static HTML at build time. The site carries ISSN
      <span class="num">{OB_ISSN}</span> and is published online only.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
